START-HERE -- things to type, and how to get back out====================================================Programs that run with nothing set up: no arguments, no data to find, noterminal type to get right.  `man <name>' tells you about any of them.PRINTS SOMETHING AND STOPS  fortune        a quotation  cookie         another, from a different collection  cal            this month, and the months either side  ascii          the character set, four ways  today          the date in words, with the phase of the moon  banner HELLO   large letters  ttyexp         a firework, then a clean screenfortune and cookie are usenet collections from the 1980s, as they arrived.RUNS UNTIL YOU STOP IT -- Ctrl-C  rain           rain on the screen  worms          worms across it  life           Conway's lifeTAKES A WHILE  advent         Colossal Cave.  Type WEST to begin.  hack           the dungeon.  `?' for keys; `Q' then `y' to quit.  larn           another dungeon.  Ctrl-C to quit.  bog            Boggle.  Ctrl-C to quit.  backgammon     it explains itself if you ask for instructions  card           a Christmas card carried by a Towers of Hanoi, twelve                 disks for twelve lines: 4095 moves.  Ctrl-C when done.Every quit key above is a play-test run against this disk before it ships.If a program holds the terminal and Ctrl-C does not reach it, Ctrl-E thenCtrl-C is os9exec's own interrupt.NEXT  man -k <word>      programs about <word>  DOC/CATEGORIES     everything, by what it is for  DOC/INDEX          everything, A to Z  keep <name>        copy one onto your own disk (DOC/README-KEEP)